</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since Best-of-N (BoN) sampling enhances test-time exploration by generating multiple solution trajectories, increasing the value of <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> can further improve the performance of the LLM-based CO solver. To evaluate the impact of <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> of BoN sampling, we conduct a sensitivity analysis on it based on the instances with large graphs, which can be more challenging due to the larger problem scale. Specifically, we vary <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> across {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64} and compare the model performance in terms of both feasibility and optimality. As shown in <a href="#A5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ E.6 Scaling Test-time Exploration ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiment Results ‣ Large Language Models as End-to-end Combinatorial Optimization Solvers" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 8</span></a>, performance improves consistently with larger values of <math id="A5.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. These results highlight that the scale of test-time exploration is a critical factor in achieving a balance between solution quality and computational efficiency, which is an important consideration for practical deployment in real-world decision-making scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
